                      %semantics
                        %mrow
                          %mn 2
                          %mi O
                        %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} 2O
                  %td{:style => "text-align: left;"}
                    %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/E7"} E7
                %tr
                  %td{:style => "text-align: left;"}
                    %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                      %semantics
                        %mrow
                          %msub
                            %mi E
                            %mn 8
                        %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} E_8
                  %td{:style => "text-align: left;"}
                    %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/dodecahedron"}> dodecahedron
                    ,
                    %br/
                    %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/icosahedron"} icosahedron
                  %td{:style => "text-align: left;"}
                    %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/icosahedral+group"} icosahedral group
                    %br/
                    %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                      %semantics
                        %mrow
                          %mi I
                        %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} I
                  %td{:style => "text-align: left;"}
                    %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/binary+icosahedral+group"} binary icosahedral group
                    %br/
                    %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                      %semantics
                        %mrow
                          %mn 2
                          %mi I
                        %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} 2I
                  %td{:style => "text-align: left;"}
                    %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/E8"} E8
          %p
            The obvious question for what might be the conceptual origin of this joint classification is attributed to (
            %a{:href => "#Arnold76"}> Arnold 76
            ).
          %p
            Starting with (
            %a{:href => "#DouglasMoore96"}> Douglas-Moore 96
            ) is the observation that many of these structures are naturally aspects of the description of
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/string+theory"} string theory
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/KK-compactification"} KK-compactified
            on
            %em
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/orbifolds"} orbifolds
              with
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/ADE+singularities"} ADE singularities
            of the form
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msup
                    %mi ℂ
                    %mi n
                  %mo ⫽
                  %mi Γ
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathbb{C}^n \sslash \Gamma
            for
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi Γ
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \Gamma
            a
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/finite+group"} finite
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/subgroup"} subgroup
            of
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msub
                      %mi SL
                      %mn 2
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi ℂ
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} SL_2(\mathbb{C})
          %h2#ViaSuperYangMillsTheory
            Via
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi N
                  %mo =
                  %mn 2
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} N=2
            super Yang-Mills theory
          %p
            Various seemingly unrelated structures in mathematics fall into an “ADE classification”. Notably
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/finite+group"} finite
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/subgroups"} subgroups
            of
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/special+unitary+group"} SU(2)
            and
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/compact+Lie+group"} compact
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/simple+Lie+groups"} simple Lie groups
            do. The way this works usually is that one tries to classify these structures somehow, and ends up finding that the classification is governed by the combinatorics of
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Dynkin+diagrams"} Dynkin diagrams
            (see also
            = succeed ")." do
              %em
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/McKay+correspondence"} McKay correspondence
          %p
            While that does explain a bit, it seems the statement that both the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/icosahedral+group"} icosahedral group
            and the Lie group
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/E8"} E8
            are related to the same
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Dynkin+diagram"} Dynkin diagram
            somehow is still more a question than an answer. Why is that so?
          %p
            The first key insight is due to
            = succeed "." do
              %a{:href => "ADE+singularity#Kronheimer89a"} Kronheimer 89
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/orbifold"} orbifold
            quotients
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msup
                    %mi ℂ
                    %mn 2
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} /
                  %mi Γ
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathbb{C}^2/\Gamma
            for finite subgroups
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi Γ
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \Gamma
            of
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi SU
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mn 2
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} SU(2)
            are precisely the generic form of the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/gauge+group"} gauge
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/orbits"} orbits
            of the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/direct+product+group"} direct product group
            of
            = succeed "s" do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi U
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %msub
                      %mi n
                      %mi i
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} U(n_i)
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/direct+sum"} direct sum
            of
            = succeed "-s," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi Hom
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %msup
                      %mi ℂ
                      %mrow
                        %msub
                          %mi n
                          %mi i
                    %mo ,
                    %msup
                      %mi ℂ
                      %mrow
                        %msub
                          %mi n
                          %mi j
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} Hom(\mathbb{C}^{n_i}, \mathbb{C}^{n_j})
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi i
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} i
            and
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi j
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} j
            range over the vertices of the
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Dynkin+diagram"} Dynkin diagram
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi i
                  %mo ,
                  %mi j
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} (i,j)
            over its edges.
          %p
            This becomes more illuminating when interpreted in terms of
            = succeed ":" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/gauge+theory"} gauge theory
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/quiver+gauge+theory"} quiver gauge theory
            the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/gauge+group"} gauge group
            is a
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/direct+product+group"} direct product group
            of
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi U
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %msub
                    %mi n
                    %mi i
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} U(n_i)
            factors associated with vertices of a
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/quiver"} quiver
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/particles"} particles
            which are
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/charged+particle"} charged
            under this gauge group arrange, as a
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/linear+representation"} linear representation
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/direct+sum"} direct sum
            of
            = succeed "-s," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi Hom
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %msup
                      %mi ℂ
                      %mrow
                        %msub
                          %mi n
                          %mi i
                    %mo ,
                    %msup
                      %mi ℂ
                      %mrow
                        %msub
                          %mi n
                          %mi j
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} Hom(\mathbb{C}^{n_i}, \mathbb{C}^{n_j})
          %p
            Pick one such particle, and follow it around as the gauge group transforms it. The space swept out is its gauge
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/orbit"} orbit
            %a{:href => "#Kronheimer89"} Kronheimer 89
            says that if the quiver is a Dynkin diagram, then this gauge orbit looks like
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msup
                      %mi ℂ
                      %mn 2
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} /
                    %mi Γ
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathbb{C}^2/\Gamma
          %p
            On the other extreme, gauge theories are of interest whose gauge group is not a big direct product, but is a
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/simple+Lie+group"} simple Lie group
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/special+unitary+group"} SU(N)
            or
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/E8"} E8
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/spontaneous+symmetry+breaking"} spontaneous symmetry breaking
            (“
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Higgs+field"}> Higgsing
            ”): the ground state energy of the field theory may happen to be achieved by putting the fields at any one point in a higher dimensional space of field configurations, acted on by the gauge group, and fixing any one such point “spontaneously” singles out the corresponding
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/stabilizer+subgroup"} stabilizer subgroup
          %p
            Now here is the final ingredient: it is
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/N%3D2+D%3D4+super+Yang-Mills+theory"} N=2 D=4 super Yang-Mills theory
            (“
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Seiberg-Witten+theory"}> Seiberg-Witten theory
            ”) which have a potential that is such that its
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/vacua"} vacua
            break a simple gauge group such as
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi SU
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi N
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} SU(N)
            down to a Dynkin diagram
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/quiver+gauge+theory"} quiver gauge theory
            = succeed "." do
              %a{:href => "N=2+D=4+super+Yang-Mills+theory#Albertsson03"} Albertsson 03, section 2.3.4
          %p
            More precisely, these theories have two different kinds of vacua, those on the “
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Coulomb+branch"}> Coulomb branch
            ” and those on the “
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Higgs+branch"}> Higgs branch
            ” depending on whether the scalars of the “
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/vector+multiplets"}> vector multiplets
            ” (the gauge field sector) or of the “
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/hypermultiplet"}> hypermultiplet
            ” (the matter field sector) vanish. The statement above is for the Higgs branch, but the Coulomb branch is supposed to behave “dually”.
          %p
            So that then finally is the relation, in the ADE classification, between the simple Lie groups and the finite subgroups of SU(2): start with an N=2 super Yang Mills theory with gauge group a simple Lie group. Let it spontaneously find its vacuum and consider the orbit space of the remaining spontaneously broken symmetry group. That is (a resolution of) the orbifold quotient of
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msup
                    %mi ℂ
                    %mn 2
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathbb{C}^2
            by a discrete subgroup of
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi SU
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mn 2
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} SU(2)
          %h2#related_concepts Related concepts
          %ul
            %li
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/24"} 24
            %li
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/McKay+correspondence"} McKay correspondence
          %h2#references References
          %h3#general_surveys General Surveys
          %ul
            %li#Arnold76
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Vladimir+Arnold"}> Vladimir Arnold
              ,
              = succeed "," do
                %em Problems in present day mathematics
              = succeed "," do
                %em Mathematical developments arising from Hilbert problems
              %em Problem VIII. The A-D-E classifications
              (V. Arnold).
          %p A survey is in
          %ul
            %li
              Michael Hazewinkel, W Hesseling, Dirk Siersma, and Ferdinand Veldkamp,
              = succeed "," do
                %em The ubiquity of Coxeter Dynkin diagrams (an introduction to the ADE problem)
              = succeed ")" do
                %a{:href => "http://oai.cwi.nl/oai/asset/10039/10039A.pdf"} pdf
          %p which in turn is summarized in
          %ul
            %li#Siegel14
              Kyler Siegel,
              %em The Ubiquity of the ADE classification in Nature
              , 2014 (
              %a{:href => "http://math.stanford.edu/~ksiegel/ADEClassifications.pdf"}> pdf
              )
          %p See also
          %ul
            %li
              Wikipedia,
              %em
                %a{:href => "http://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/ADE_classification"} ADE classification
          %p Discussion of the free finite group actions on spheres goes back to
          %ul
            %li#Milnor57
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/John+Milnor"}> John Milnor
              ,
              = succeed "," do
                %em
                  Groups which act on
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %msup
                          %mi S
                          %mi n
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} S^n
                  without fixed points
              = succeed ")" do
                %a{:href => "http://www.jstor.org/stable/2372566"} JSTOR
          %p Review inclues
          %ul
            %li#Keenan03
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Adam+Keenan"}> Adam Keenan
              ,
              = succeed "," do
                %em Which finite groups act freely on spheres?
              = succeed ")" do
                %a{:href => "http://www.math.utah.edu/~keenan/actions.pdf"} pdf
          %p
            Discussion of
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/ALE+spaces"} ALE spaces
            via ADE include
          %ul
            %li#Kronheimer89
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Peter+Kronheimer"}> Peter Kronheimer
              ,
              = succeed "," do
                %em The construction of ALE spaces as hyper-Kähler quotients
              = succeed ")" do
                %a{:href => "https://projecteuclid.org/euclid.jdg/1214443066"} Euclid
          %p Related stuff includes…
          %p
            on
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/immersions"} immersions
            of
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/3-spheres"} 3-spheres
            into
            = succeed ":" do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msup
                      %mi ℝ
                      %mn 4
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathbb{R}^4
          %ul
            %li
              Shumi Kinjo,
              %em Immersions of 3-sphere into 4-space associated with Dynkin diagrams of types A and D
              (
              %a{:href => "http://arxiv.org/abs/1309.6526"}> arXiv:1309.6526
              )
          %h3#in_string_theory In string theory
          %p
            The original articles explaining the appearance of ADE classification from within
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/string+theory"} string theory
            include
          %ul
            %li#DouglasMoore96
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Michael+Douglas"}> Michael Douglas
                ,
                = succeed "," do
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Gregory+Moore"} Gregory Moore
                %em D-branes, Quivers, and ALE Instantons
                (
                %a{:href => "http://arxiv.org/abs/hep-th/9603167"}> arXiv:hep-th/9603167
                )
            %li
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Clifford+Johnson"}> Clifford Johnson
                ,
                = succeed "," do
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Robert+Myers"} Robert Myers
                = succeed "," do
                  %em Aspects of Type IIB Theory on ALE Spaces
                = succeed ")" do
                  %a{:href => "http://arxiv.org/abs/hep-th/9610140"} arXiv:hep-th/9610140
            %li
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Michael+Douglas"}> Michael Douglas
                ,
                = succeed "," do
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Brian+Greene"} Brian Greene
                = succeed "," do
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/David+Morrison"} David Morrison
                = succeed "," do
                  %em Orbifold Resolution by D-Branes
                = succeed ")" do
                  %a{:href => "http://arxiv.org/abs/hep-th/9704151"} arXiv:hep-th/9704151
            %li
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Brian+Greene"}> Brian Greene
                ,
                = succeed "," do
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Calin+Lazaroiu"} Calin Lazaroiu
                = succeed "," do
                  %em D-branes on Nonabelian Threefold Quotient Singularities
                = succeed ")" do
                  %a{:href => "http://arxiv.org/abs/hep-th/9811201"} arXiv:hep-th/9811201
            %li
              %p
                Andrea Cappelli, Jean-Bernard Zuber,
                %em A-D-E Classification of Conformal Field Theories
                (
                %a{:href => "http://arxiv.org/abs/0911.3242"}> arXiv:0911.3242
                )
          %p Surveys include
          %ul
            %li
              MO discussion,
              %em
                %a{:href => "http://mathoverflow.net/a/34680/381"} ADE classification from string theory
          %p
            Discussion of an ADE-classification of BPS
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Freund-Rubin+compactifications"} Freund-Rubin compactifications
            is in
          %ul
            %li#MFFGME09
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Paul+de+Medeiros"}> Paul de Medeiros
              ,
              = succeed "," do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Jos%C3%A9+Figueroa-O%27Farrill"} José Figueroa-O'Farrill
              = succeed "," do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Sunil+Gadhia"} Sunil Gadhia
              = succeed "," do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Elena+M%C3%A9ndez-Escobar"} Elena Méndez-Escobar
              = succeed "," do
                %em Half-BPS quotients in M-theory: ADE with a twist
              = succeed "," do
                %a{:href => "http://arxiv.org/abs/0909.0163"} arXiv:0909.0163
              = succeed ")" do
                %a{:href => "http://www.maths.ed.ac.uk/~jmf/CV/Seminars/YRM2010.pdf"} pdf slides
          %p
            Specifically the ADE classfication involved in the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/6d+%282%2C0%29-supersymmetric+QFT"} 6d (2,0)-supersymmetric QFT
            on the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/M5-brane"} M5-brane
            is discussed in
          %ul
            %li#Witten95
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Edward+Witten"}> Edward Witten
                ,
                %em Some Comments On String Dynamics
                (
                %a{:href => "http://arxiv.org/abs/hep-th/9507121"}> arXiv:hep-th/9507121
                )
            %li#HeckmannMorrisonVafa13
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Jonathan+Heckman"}> Jonathan Heckman
                ,
                = succeed "," do
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/David+Morrison"} David Morrison
                = succeed "," do
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Cumrun+Vafa"} Cumrun Vafa
                %em On the Classification of 6D SCFTs and Generalized ADE Orbifolds
                (
                %a{:href => "http://arxiv.org/abs/1312.5746"}> arXiv:1312.5746
                )
          %p
            Discussion in the context of
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/M-theory+on+G%E2%82%82-manifolds"} M-theory on G₂-manifolds
            includes
          %ul
            %li#Acharya02
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Bobby+Acharya"}> Bobby Acharya
                , section 3.1.1 of
                = succeed "," do
                  %em
                    M Theory,
                    = succeed "-manifolds" do
                      %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                        %semantics
                          %mrow
                            %msub
                              %mi G
                              %mn 2
                          %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} G_2
                = succeed ")" do
                  %a{:href => "http://users.ictp.it/~pub_off/lectures/lns013/Acharya/Acharya_Final.pdf"} pdf
            %li#BBS07
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Katrin+Becker"}> Katrin Becker
                ,
                = succeed "," do
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Melanie+Becker"} Melanie Becker
                = succeed "," do
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/John+Schwarz"} John Schwarz
                = succeed "," do
                  %em String Theory and M-Theory: A Modern Introduction
          %h3#ReferencesInSolidStatePhysics In solid state physics
          %p
            On ADE-classifications in/of
            = succeed "/" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/topological+phases+of+matter"} topological phases of matter
            = succeed ":" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/topological+order"} topological order
          %ul
            %li
              Mayukh Nilay Khan, Jeffrey C. Y. Teo, Taylor L. Hughes,
              = succeed "," do
                %em Anyonic Symmetries and Topological Defects in Abelian Topological Phases: an application to the ADE Classification
              %strong 90
              235149 (2014)
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} [
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} [
              %a{:href => "https://arxiv.org/abs/1403.6478"} arXiv:1403.6478
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
